The Bridge Pattern.

Since you mentioned early on that your architectural philosophy is "Composition over Inheritance" (Pillar 4: Loose
Coupling), this pattern is going to feel incredibly satisfying.

The Bridge Pattern is the ultimate weapon against the "Cartesian Product Subclass Explosion."
The Problem: The Orthogonal Dimensions

Imagine you are building an Enterprise Notification System.
You have two types of Notifications (The Abstraction): Standard and Urgent.
You have two delivery methods (The Implementation): SMS and Email.

The Beginner Way:
The Junior Developer uses Inheritance (extends) for everything. To cover all combinations, they create:

    1. StandardSmsNotification
    2. UrgentSmsNotification
    3. StandardEmailNotification
    4. UrgentEmailNotification

Why a Senior rejects this:
This is mathematically disastrous. If the boss says, "Add a 'Marketing' notification type, and add 'WhatsApp' as a
delivery method," you don't just add two classes. You have to multiply!
3 Types × 3 Methods = 9 separate classes.
Add a 4th of each, and you have 16 classes. Your architecture collapses under its own weight.
The Senior Solution: The Bridge Pattern

The Bridge Pattern says: "Stop tying the 'What' (Abstraction) to the 'How' (Implementation) using inheritance.
Separate them into two distinct hierarchies, and put a Bridge (Composition) between them."

Instead of creating 16 classes, we create just 4 classes and link them together at runtime.
Adding a new delivery method doesn't require changing any of the notification types!